Date: Sat, 13 Nov 1993 19:13:44 -0800
From: kwatson@netcom.com (Kennita Watson)
Subject: Extropians

Hal Finney says:
   I don't know why it is that extropianism, a philosophy which in my mind
   promotes tolerance and diversity, seems to attract the intolerant and
   argumentative.

I've wondered why this seems to be the case as well, and also wondered
it about the Libertarian mailing list I was once on.  I have a few
possible explanations, which I believe apply to both (overlapping)
groups.  Unless otherwise noted, I refer to both groups when I speak
of Extropians.

I will make the following comment that speaks to my observation before
I go on. I am aware that as with all generalizations, there are
counterexamples to what I say here -- I don't think I need to be
informed of them.  Please trust that what I say here I say with the
hope that some good (insight, positive discussion, etc.) will come of
it.  As you read, please take it in that spirit, and remember that
we're on the same side.

1) Extropians tend to be highly intelligent, and very well-read even
when not highly educated.  As such, they attend more to their minds
than to their hearts, and to dialectical and rhetorical skills rather
than to social graces.  Thus, even statements that are not meant to be 
inflammatory can be interpreted as such due to lack of customary
"semantic sugar" (my own term).  Indeed, some may feel that such
niceties are unnecessary among intelligent, reasoning beings, and thus
disdain them.  But until we are uploaded we get to be mammals, and
even after that we will probably have emotions, and thus have
reactions to the world around us (and to the words that make up so 
much of that world for many of us) that are all-but-unrelated to our
rational assessments.

2) Extropians tend to be highly individualistic -- indeed, many of
them may have grown up in a society of one, feeling isolated and
out-of-place in families and groups that did not share their interests
or vision.  This can develop into a habit of looking at everyone as
being on the other side:  of assuming that any disagreement is an
attack, because it so often was.  When one's world view and sense of
self are based on a feeling of isolation, it can be intoxicating but
frightening to suddenly be a member of a community.  The hindbrain
"don't change anything that kept you alive this long" defenses would
kick in to push this community away and assert the familiar
society-of-one status quo.

3) Being fairly rational people, Extropians want to make a difference
when they say something.  This means not wasting bandwidth or time by
tossing many comments like "I agree!" into a discussion.
Unfortunately, this can end up looking like this bizarre twist on a
mother's advice:  "If you can't say something argumentative, don't say
anything at all.".

4) Some Extropians, particularly a brand of Objectivists with which I
identify, have their self-image very tightly bound up with their
rationality.  In order to think well of themselves, they need not only
to have the right answer on any given question, but to be able to
prove to themselves and to others that they came by that answer in a 
quintessentially rational manner.  This comes largely from the concept
that irrationality is the root of all evil, and the conviction that
rationality is the only valid basis for esteem, including self-esteem.

5) Some Extropians seem (out of a love of consistency, and perhaps a
certain naivete about the contrariness of the universe) not to have
learned a lesson that has been taught by fairy-tale writers since time
immemorial and brought to mind by James Bond:  Never say "Never" (or
"Always" or "No matter what" or...) -- life is sure to toss you a
"Sometimes" or a "Sometimes not" or a "What if...?" to make you sorry
you ever said anything.  Thus the broad-statement/counterexample wars.

6) Since the traffic on this list and others is heavy, people in
general don't bother posting unless they feel strongly about the
subject at hand.  Note that the strength of their feeling is
independent of any objective assessment of their ability to defend
their position convincingly.  This is a prescription for heated
discussion.

7) Miscellaneous: there are those who can't resist fighting losing
battles, those who haven't come to terms with how intelligent they are
and so feel a need to make others look stupid, those who crave
attention so much that they will start a fight to get some, those who 
have fought so many losing battles on physical battlefields that they
lust for victory on the battlefield of words, and so on.  I'm sure
there are more -- there are an awful lot of possibilities.

Seeing one or more of these things in others or in ourselves, what do
we do?  Maybe be a little more compassionate and considerate of
others' feelings, realizing that people are taking a risk by exposing
their ideas to public scrutiny.  Maybe decide not to pick up a
gauntlet or two that has been thrown down.  Maybe offer some
encouragement and support (privately in email if not to the list) to
someone who says something with which we agree, instead of only
responding when something rubs us the wrong way.  There are more of
these, too, but I have to leave _something_ as an exercise for the
student :-) .

In friendship,
Kennita

