Date: Sat, 13 Nov 93 19:22:52 PST
From: mlinksva@netcom.com (Michael R Linksvayer)
Subject: Meta: Ponzi, truth, reputations, twits

If one does not believe that cryonics will ever work (as many
reasonable people do, not including me, but then I don't claim to be a
reasonable person) then it is perfectly reasonable to claim that anyone
behind a cryonics organization is involved in a ponzi scheme---they
keep on taking money, then when cryonics is disproven, whoever is still
around is left holding the bag.

If seen in this light, James Donald is not guilty of defamation any
more than most of us are when we accuse Congress, the AARP or whoever
of being involved in a ponzi scheme with regards to Social Security.

But I'm certainly not going to spend any more time defending James
Donald, who I think is a twit.

However, I do think that some people on this list do have their heads
up their asses.

It's nice that we're conducting an experiment in social dynamics (or
whatever the proper term is) here, for it is obvious that we are
proving ourselves right---totalitarianism doesn't work.

We need market-based mechanisms. Exclusion is a start, ratings, if
properly implemented, could be another step in the right direction.
The whole list "justice" mechanism should be smashed to tiny bits.
Anarchy!

And what about our pitiful reputations? Well, in the not very distant
future it's often going to be very hard to tell truth from fiction.
Outmoded "justice" systems will never be able to cope. I don't know
what we will use to preserve our reputations, but I'll bet 5000 thornes
(gee, I don't have 5000 pounds) it's more likely to do with cryptostuff
than petty tribunals.

Mike Linksvayer   mlinksva@netcom.com

