Subject: Response to Nan Wolfslayer

>From: LEVY%BESSIE@venus.cis.yale.edu
>X-Message-Number: #93-11-330
>
> ...
>By *synthetic* I mean a "bottom-up" approach that avoids positing abstract units, 
>and sticks instead to physically observable quantities, like energy flow, trying 
>to model how the physics could account for the same behavior that the analytical 
>approach tries to handle, without all the ontological baggage.  This has been a 
>paradigm in connectionist modelling for years, and, though I disagree with the 
>methodology and arguments of many connectionist researchers, I consider the 
>enterprise itself to be more promising than the traditional symbol-based 
>alternatives. 
>
> ...
>
>To me, the only really interesting question is: "How can things like life and 
>language have developed from inanimate matter?"  Coming up with more an more 
>theories about what life and language are "really like" isn't going to answer 
>that question.  Trying to *model* how life and language could have arisen looks 
>like a more promising alternative.  ...
>

I don't usually post "I agree!" messages, but here I have to make an exception.

I agree!

And I would also like to point out that this bottom-up approach has another
powerful advantage.  It allows one to break the problem (of life and mind)
along phylogenetic boundaries instead of along functional subsystems.  I mean
that the traditional approach, in order to even start at the problem, forces
one to simplify it by studying subsystems of the brain in isolation from
each other and from the more fundamental task of survival.  Thus most research,
be it in neurophysiology, psychology, or computer science, studies postulated
(perceptual, motor, cognitive, memory, etc.) subsystems in isolation,
preferring usually to model the vast complexity of primate nervous systems.
Then the task is to "just put it all together".  I'd argue that putting it all
together is the gist of the problem and cannot be postponed...

The bottom-up approach, on the other hand, allows one to study an entire
organism at once, starting with simple organisms (capable of little more than
prewired taxic behavior) and progressing through stages of complexity only
after understanding the reasons for the complexity.  Thus, we should not
postulate "representational memory" until we find the need for it, and many
apparently complex and adaptive behaviors do not require such a system.  The
bottom-up approach is often neglected simply because science usually proceeds
through a reductionist methodology that is more applicable to issues in
physics.  In studying the brain we should adopt a strategy that makes use of
the fact that it evolved.

Paul


